opd: take the OPD KL's student log-probs from the trainer, not from the ratio.

--use-rollout-logprobs selects one tensor that is then used for two unrelated
jobs (loss.py): the denominator of the importance ratio, and the student side of
the OPD reverse KL, which *is* the advantage.

Those want different tensors. Importance sampling gives

    KL(pi || pi_T) = E_{y~mu}[ (pi(y)/mu(y)) * (log pi(y) - log pi_T(y)) ]

so the ratio's denominator should be mu -- the policy that sampled the tokens,
i.e. the rollout engine's log-probs -- while the quantity it weights should come
from the current policy. With --opd-type megatron the teacher side is always a
Megatron forward, so taking the student side from SGLang puts cross-engine
kernel disagreement straight into the advantage; taking both from the same
forward cancels it. Turning --use-rollout-logprobs off instead fixes the KL but
silently drops the off-policy correction the async loop needs.

Two one-line changes:

  loss.py   the OPD call reads rollout_data["log_probs"] (the trainer's own
            forward) and falls back to the ratio's tensor when absent.
  actor.py  that forward is also run when --use-rollout-logprobs is set, if OPD
            is on. can_reuse_log_probs_in_loss is already hard-false whenever
            use_opd is set, so this cannot make it run twice.

Both are gated on args.use_opd, so runs without OPD are unaffected.

